WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:04.800
Was it the action? The writing? The side&nbsp;
characters? Fans who've gone to see "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:07.860
Dial of Destiny" all seem to agree&nbsp;
that the movie got one thing wrong.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:12.480
The "Indiana Jones" franchise has returned with&nbsp;
its fifth and potentially final installment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:17.400
"Indiana Jones and the Dial of Destiny." The new&nbsp;
film is, notably, the first in the series' history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:21.660
not to be directed by Steven Spielberg,&nbsp;
who stepped aside this time around to let&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:26.460
"Logan" and "Ford v. Ferrari" filmmaker James&nbsp;
Mangold sit in the director's chair. Despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:30.360
Spielberg's absence, "The Dial of Destiny"&nbsp;
does feature the return of Harrison Ford.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:34.380
In the weeks since it made its premiere at the&nbsp;
Cannes International Film Festival, the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:38.940
has inspired plenty of passionate responses.&nbsp;
Now that it's playing in theaters nationwide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:43.740
the conversations surrounding it have only&nbsp;
intensified, too. While the film has so far been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:48.960
received fairly positively, many longtime "Indiana&nbsp;
Jones" fans have taken to social media to explain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:53.640
why they were let down by the visual effects and&nbsp;
CGI featured throughout "The Dial of Destiny."

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:56.520
On Reddit, one fan pointed&nbsp;
specifically to the film's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:59.220
Nazi Germany-set opening flashback, writing,

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.740
"The HORRIBLE CGI when Indy was running on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:05.460
top of the train in the intro had me&nbsp;
shaking my head almost immediately."

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:07.920
In the same Reddit thread, another viewer wrote,

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.020
"Every time they went to CGI when they could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:12.660
have used practical effects […]&nbsp;
it was a little disappointing."

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:16.800
Fans haven't just taken issue with the&nbsp;
CGI used in "Indiana Jones and the Dial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.180
of Destiny." Viewers have also expressed&nbsp;
their dissatisfaction online with many of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:24.660
the film's action sequences. In one&nbsp;
Reddit thread, a user even asked,

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:28.980
"I saw this on IMAX. Is it just me or were the&nbsp;
hand-to-hand fight sequences edited to s—?"

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:33.060
Their comment received numerous&nbsp;
passionate responses from other fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:34.800
including one person who wrote,

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:39.060
"It felt so claustrophobic. Visually&nbsp;
it just looked wrong most of the time."

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:44.160
Another "Indiana Jones" fan offered their critique&nbsp;
of the film's various action moments, noting,

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:48.840
"We almost never got a chance to soak in the set&nbsp;
pieces because there were hardly any wide shots."

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:52.260
It's not hard to see why fans are going&nbsp;
out of their way to hold "The Dial of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:56.340
Destiny" to such a high standard, either.&nbsp;
The first three "Indiana Jones" movies are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:59.520
widely considered some of the greatest&nbsp;
action films that have ever been made.

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:01.200
*Sword moves, Indy shoots him*

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:08.640
Steven Spielberg's artful, exuberant&nbsp;
direction of them has, consequently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:13.500
been praised quite a bit over the years by&nbsp;
critics, casual fans, and even other filmmakers.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:17.220
Of course, James Mangold also knew that&nbsp;
he was taking on a near-impossible task&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:19.860
when he agreed to helm the&nbsp;
film in Spielberg's place.

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:22.560
In an interview with IndieWire, Mangold spoke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:26.700
long and admiringly of Spielberg's style&nbsp;
and approach to filmmaking, explaining,

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:30.960
"Every shot has a purpose in Steven's&nbsp;
work. Single-camera filmmakers who are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:34.800
building sequences out of shots with a&nbsp;
purpose has gotten quite rare. It's all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.320
built on a powerful connection&nbsp;
to the movies he grew up with."

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:40.860
Taking that into account, when he&nbsp;
finally signed on to direct "Indiana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:45.120
Jones and the Dial of Destiny," Mangold&nbsp;
said he didn't feel any need to try to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:48.480
reinvent the formula that Spielberg&nbsp;
had previously perfected, adding,

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:53.400
"I had to screw my head on to the idea that I was&nbsp;
essentially a pinch hitter for Babe Ruth. I didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:58.020
feel like I had to play out some sort of rejection&nbsp;
of his aesthetic. I'm a profound admirer."

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:00.888
"You call this archaeology?"
"Get out of there, dad."

00:03:00.888 --> 00:03:05.100
Whether or not the "Dial of Destiny" filmmaker&nbsp;
managed to match the quality level established&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:09.540
by Spielberg is something that fans will&nbsp;
likely spend years debating. It's also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:13.500
worth noting that Spielberg's own "Kingdom of&nbsp;
the Crystal Skull" faced similar criticisms.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.800
Mangold's film doesn't seem to have&nbsp;
been received quite as poorly as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:20.340
that 2008 blockbuster. As some of&nbsp;
the above comments prove, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:24.840
that doesn't mean fans don't have a lot to say&nbsp;
about "Indiana Jones and the Dial of Destiny."

